              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 11.3: Sunlight falling on the Moon"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.341,
            0.084,
            0.8,
            0.104
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "11.1.3 Making sense of our observations"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.338,
            0.107,
            0.883,
            0.233
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The shape of the Moon itself does not change, only what we see changes. You may recall learning earlier that the Moon does not emit light of its own, but shines because it reflects sunlight that falls on it. The half of the Moon that faces the Sun receives sunlight and becomes illuminated (Fig. 11.3). The other half facing away from the Sun does not receive sunlight and remains non-illuminated."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.339,
            0.234,
            0.883,
            0.306
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The Moon revolves around the Earth and, only one half of the Moon always faces the Earth. However, the portion of the Moon facing the Earth is not always its illuminated part. We can only see the illuminated portion"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.305,
            0.884,
            0.415
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "of the Moon from Earth. Sometimes, the entire illuminated portion of the Moon faces the Earth, and at other times only a part of it. At such times the illuminated portion of the Moon that we see is not a full circle. On New Moon day, we do not see the illuminated portion of the Moon at all, as only the non-illuminated portion of the Moon faces the Earth. Therefore, the Moon appears different"
            }
          ],
          "continues_prev": true
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.048,
            0.416,
            0.163,
            0.528
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 6,
              "bbox": [
                0.048,
                0.416,
                0.163,
                0.528
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_4_image_6.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.168,
            0.424,
            0.393,
            0.512
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Why does the illuminated portion of the Moon seen from the Earth decrease when it appears closer to the Sun?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.403,
            0.413,
            0.58,
            0.432
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "on different days."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.401,
            0.433,
            0.883,
            0.507
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us do an activity to understand how the illuminated portion of the Moon, as seen by us, changes when its position changes with respect to the Sun."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.269,
            0.532,
            0.636,
            0.556
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Activity 11.2: Let us explore"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.124,
            0.588,
            0.199,
            0.734
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 11,
              "bbox": [
                0.124,
                0.588,
                0.199,
                0.734
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_4_image_11.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 14,
              "bbox": [
                0.063,
                0.74,
                0.265,
                0.757
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 11.4: (a) Ball with stick"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.273,
            0.569,
            0.88,
            0.606
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Take a small soft ball and insert a stick into it (Fig. 11.4a). This represents the Moon."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.273,
            0.607,
            0.883,
            0.68
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "z Go to a dark open place (at night), and ask a teacher or guardian to shine a torchlight towards you from about 3 m to represent light coming from the Sun or stand near an electric lamp. Your head represents the Earth."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.273,
            0.681,
            0.883,
            0.753
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "z Now hold the ball at arm’s length in one hand such that it is slightly above your head as shown in Fig. 11.4b. Keep the ball at position E towards the direction of the lamp. Does the portion of the ball facing you appear to be illuminated or not?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.271,
            0.754,
            0.883,
            0.844
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Turn around slowly, in the anti-clockwise direction, with your arm outstretched as shown in Fig. 11.4b and keep looking at the ball. Does the shape of the illuminated portion change? Is the line separating the illuminated and non-illuminated portions of the ball curved?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.273,
            0.845,
            0.885,
            0.92
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "z Was your observation similar to the changing shape of the illuminated portion of ball shown in Fig. 11.4c? The shape of the illuminated portion of the ball, as seen by you, changes depending on where the ball is with respect to the lamp."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
